-\subsection*{Strategy of the proof}
-In the algebraic setting, the theorem of \cite{ELMNP09} rests on the
-extension of sections of line bundles from $V$ to $X$; no such tool
-is available for transcendental classes. Our argument replaces it by the
-trace operator of \cite{DX24}, which produces from a potential on $X$ a
-singularity type on $V$, and by an analytic gluing construction which
-goes in the opposite direction, from a K\"ahler current on $V$ to a
-K\"ahler current on $X$. The argument is by contradiction. Assume that $\alpha$ is big and that
-$V$ is an irreducible component of ${E_{\mathrm{nK}}}(\alpha)$, not contained in the
-non-nef locus ${E_{\mathrm{nn}}}(\alpha)$, with
-$h\coloneqq \langle\alpha^{\dim V}\rangle_{X\mid V}>0$. After an embedded
-resolution we may assume that $V$ is smooth. The proof then proceeds in
-four steps.
-\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=*,itemsep=.2em,topsep=.3em]
-\item By the trace formula of \cite{DX24,SGPT}, the number $h$ is the
-  non-pluripolar mass, on $V$, of the trace $u$ of a potential with
-  minimal singularities in $\alpha$. Restricting Demailly approximants
-  of this potential to $V$ produces potentials
-  $\phi_m$ whose masses tend to $h$, which are less singular than
-  $u$ with respect to the $P$-preorder for
-  large $m$, and whose singularities are exactly the
-  restrictions to $V$ of the ambient multiplier ideals
-  $\mathcal I(mV_\theta)$ (\cref{nk:sec:setup}).
-\item A quantitative domination lemma (\cref{nk:lem:domination}), based on
-  the bigness criterion of Xiao and Popovici for differences of nef
-  classes \cite{Xiao15,Pop16}, converts the positive mass into a
-  K\"ahler current $K$ on
-  $V$ whose singularities dominate those of an auxiliary potential
-  built from $u$ and $\phi_m$ by a rooftop construction. This yields a K\"ahler current on $V$ with
-  analytic singularities whose multiplier ideals are related to the
-  restricted ambient multiplier ideals by explicit inclusions
-  (\cref{nk:sec:positive-current}).
-\item A principalization of the relevant ideals which is compatible with
-  the strict transform $W$ of $V$ (\cref{nk:lem:relative-principalization})
-  makes it possible to compare the singularities of the pulled-back
-  current on $W$ with those, near $W$, of an ambient K\"ahler current
-  $Q_M$ which is singular along $V$ (\cref{nk:sec:principalization}).
-  The ambient current is produced by the quantitative form of Demailly's
-  regularization theorem in \cref{nk:lem:regularization}; the current on
-  $V$, regularized at the level $\ell=M/C$, induces on $W$, after
-  a small twist by exceptional divisors, a K\"ahler class.
-\item This class is converted into a K\"ahler current on a neighborhood
-  of $W$ whose potential is dominated, away from $W$, by that of the
-  pulled-back ambient current, and the two are glued by an elementary
-  maximum construction (\cref{nk:prop:gluing}). The resulting
-  K\"ahler current is smooth near some point of $W$, which contradicts
-  the transformation rule for the non-K\"ahler locus under modifications
-  (\cref{nk:sec:gluing}).
-\end{enumerate}
-
-Two features of the argument deserve emphasis. First, all comparisons
-between singularities on $V$ and ambient singularities are mediated by
-the restrictions to $V$ of the ambient multiplier ideals
-$\mathcal I(mV_\theta)$, rather than by the multiplier ideals of the trace
-itself; it is this that makes the divisorial bookkeeping on a
-modification adapted to $V$ possible. Second, the construction runs at
-two levels $m\ll M$: every constant entering the positivity estimates
-is fixed before the level $M$ is chosen, while singularities are
-compared at the much finer level $M$. The quantitative form of Demailly's
-regularization theorem proved in \cref{nk:lem:regularization}, with a loss
-of positivity of order $1/M$, is what allows the two levels to
-interact.
-
